arm64: dts: h713: reserve the scanout IOVA range for the VE

Sustained decoding fails after roughly 35 minutes.  A two-hour soak ran 1683
iterations clean, then began failing -- first only the 1080p vector, within
minutes every vector including 352x288, at which point ~40% of iterations were
failing and the rate was still climbing.  Each failure is an md5 mismatch, but
the mismatch is a symptom: the decode is truncated because buffer allocation
failed partway through.

    sun50i-iommu 2010000.iommu: iova 0x000000006c800000 already mapped to
        0x000000006c800000 cannot remap to 0x0000000040e21000 prot: 0x3
    cedrus 1c0e000.video-codec: dma alloc of size 3133440 failed

It is not memory pressure.  MemAvailable oscillates with no trend and returns
to its baseline afterwards, CmaFree is constant to the kilobyte across the
whole run, buddyinfo shows 61 free order-10 blocks at the moment of failure,
and there is no allocation-failure or OOM message anywhere.

Every one of the 606 colliding IOVAs falls inside 0x6c100000-0x6c8fffff, which
is uboot-scanout@6c100000: the 8 MiB framebuffer the display adopts from U-Boot
and identity-maps so the panel can scan out of it.  The VE and the display sit
in the same IOMMU group and so share one IOVA space.  Nothing withheld that
range from the VE's DMA allocator, so the allocator eventually reached it, and
from then on every allocation landing there was refused -- permanently, because
an identity mapping is not transient.

The reservation that should have prevented this exists but does not reach the
VE.  of_iommu_get_resv_regions() only produces a region for a device when that
device lists the node in its own memory-region AND the node's iommu-addresses
names that device's phandle; uboot-scanout named only &display.  The group-wide
view hides this, because /sys/kernel/iommu_groups/0/reserved_regions aggregates
across the group and duly showed the range -- while the allocator the VE
actually draws from had never heard of it.

That matters more than it looks, because iova_reserve_iommu_regions() runs for
exactly one device per domain: the first to initialise it.  Every later device
hits

    /* start_pfn is always nonzero for an already-initialised domain */
    if (iovad->start_pfn)
            return 0;

in iommu_dma_init_domain() and never contributes its regions at all.  The VE
probes first here, so the VE's regions are the only ones that were ever going
to be applied -- and it had none.

Adding a second reserved-memory node, with no "reg", gives the VE an
IOMMU_RESV_RESERVED for the same range: the IOVA is withheld from its allocator
and nothing is mapped.  Reusing the existing node instead would not work -- it
has a "reg", so iommu_resv_region_get_type() would return IOMMU_RESV_DIRECT for
the VE as well and the direct-mapping pass would try to identity-map a range
that is already identity-mapped.  A reg-less reserved-memory node costs no
memory and produces no warning: __reserved_mem_reserve_reg() returns -ENOENT
for it silently, and with no "size" property it is not treated as a dynamic
region either.

Declaring it on both devices, rather than only on the VE, makes the fix
independent of probe order.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -116,6 +116,38 @@
 			no-map;
 			iommu-addresses = <&display 0x6c100000 0x800000>;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * The same range again, as an IOVA reservation for the VE.
+		 *
+		 * The VE and the display share one IOMMU group, and therefore
+		 * one IOVA space. The node above makes the display identity-map
+		 * this range; nothing made the VE's DMA allocator avoid it, so
+		 * after roughly 35 minutes of sustained decoding the allocator
+		 * walked into it and every allocation there failed permanently
+		 * ("iova 0x6c800000 already mapped ... cannot remap").
+		 *
+		 * It cannot be fixed by adding &ve to the node above: that node
+		 * has a "reg", so of_iommu_get_resv_regions() would classify it
+		 * IOMMU_RESV_DIRECT for the VE too and try to identity-map an
+		 * already-mapped range. A node with NO "reg" is classified
+		 * IOMMU_RESV_RESERVED instead -- the IOVA range is withheld from
+		 * the allocator and nothing is mapped, which is exactly what the
+		 * VE needs. The reserved-memory scan ignores a reg-less node
+		 * (-ENOENT, silently), so this costs no memory and warns about
+		 * nothing; it exists only as a handle for the IOMMU code.
+		 *
+		 * Both devices must declare it because the IOVA allocator is
+		 * populated per DEVICE, by whichever one initialises the shared
+		 * domain first -- every later device takes the "already
+		 * initialised" early return in iommu_dma_init_domain() and never
+		 * contributes its regions. The VE probes first today, so the VE
+		 * is the one that must carry it; declaring it on both makes the
+		 * result independent of probe order.
+		 */
+		ve_scanout_iova: iova-reserve-scanout {
+			iommu-addresses = <&ve 0x6c100000 0x800000>;
+		};
 	};
 
 	psci {
@@ -1307,6 +1339,14 @@
 			 * fault, because nothing ever touched an unmapped IOVA.
 			 */
 			iommus = <&iommu 0 1>, <&iommu 1 1>;
+			/*
+			 * Not a buffer pool -- an IOVA reservation. See
+			 * iova-reserve-scanout in reserved-memory. Nothing
+			 * claims this as DMA memory: the auto-claim in
+			 * of_dma_set_restricted_buffer() only fires for a
+			 * "restricted-dma-pool" compatible, which this is not.
+			 */
+			memory-region = <&ve_scanout_iova>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
